TY  - JOUR
TI  - Learning to Reason with Neural Networks: Generalization, Unseen Data and Boolean Measures
AU  - Emmanuel Abbe
AU  - Samy Bengio
AU  - Elisabetta Cornacchia
AU  - Jon M. Kleinberg
AU  - Aryo Lotfi
AU  - Maithra Raghu
AU  - Chiyuan Zhang
PY  - 2022
AB  - This paper considers the Pointer Value Retrieval (PVR) benchmark introduced in [ZRKB21], where a `reasoning' function acts on a string of digits to produce the label. More generally, the paper considers the learning of logical functions with gradient descent (GD) on neural networks. It is first shown that in order to learn logical functions with gradient descent on symmetric neural networks, the generalization error can be lower-bounded in terms of the noise-stability of the target function, supporting a conjecture made in [ZRKB21]. It is then shown that in the distribution shift setting, when the data withholding corresponds to freezing a single feature (referred to as canonical holdout), the generalization error of gradient descent admits a tight characterization in terms of the Boolean influence for several relevant architectures. This is shown on linear models and supported experimentally on other models such as MLPs and Transformers. In particular, this puts forward the hypothesis that for such architectures and for learning logical functions such as PVR functions, GD tends to have an implicit bias towards low-degree representations, which in turn gives the Boolean influence for the generalization error under quadratic loss.
UR  - https://papers.nips.cc/paper_files/paper/2022/hash/12202970782399ee67981dc5269c3b8a-Abstract-Conference.html
